><text color="#9643FC" creationdate="D:20250624113615+10'00'" flags="print,nozoom,norotate" inreplyto="30515601-f444-44b8-9987-f97801df62fa" date="D:20250624113615+10'00'" name="8b21eaac-f4a6-4dd0-89e4-bf0d5b764e9c" icon="Comment" page="4" rect="310.539000,451.707000,334.539000,475.707000" subject="Sticky Note" title="alexd"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Most sports have a screening policy, with reasonable&#xD;uniformity of components. All included H&amp;P, and almost&#xD;all included ECG. Only one sport included an echocardiogram&#xD;and exercise stress test as a standard part of screening&#xD;(international players only). Promoting the latest ECG&#xD;interpretation criteria may reduce false-positives and cost.&#xD;Future work should explore cardiac emergency plans, infrastructure&#xD;needed for screening, cost, and long-term&#xD;follow-up.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="4" rect="593.972000,383.707000,777.972000,475.707000"
/></text
